                    {
                        "translation_id": "d575cb5e-8600-4eb5-8e13-7684e44ba328",
                        "source_entry_id": "2dd45744-32e0-3d53-85e1-e3d1653e93fa",
                        "source_sense_id": "0a8d1502-5b5d-3c81-9496-79e33bf1bb0d",
                        "source_sense_description": "en 2. (groene sprietjes van) looksoort Allium schoenoprasum",
                        "target_lang_word": "Schnittlauch",
                        "target_lang_code": "de",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "Schnittlauch",
                                "target_lang_code": "de",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "5f07de70-9f6b-39c8-9fae-c3be0026d4de",
                                        "entry_id": "7eeb95e2-c87c-368f-a58e-a93699fe79f9",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Gewürzpflanze mit grasähnlichen, röhrenartigen Blättern, die hauptsächlich kleingeschnitten im Salat verwendet werden"
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "30f30969-b17d-47de-ac9b-2ca50b9393a1",
                                                "sense_id": "5f07de70-9f6b-39c8-9fae-c3be0026d4de",
                                                "text": "Die röhrigen Blätter des Schnittlauchs sind eine beliebte Zutat für Salate und Suppen."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c0bbd64d-cc0d-4fca-adae-5811edb714ec",
                                                "sense_id": "5f07de70-9f6b-39c8-9fae-c3be0026d4de",
                                                "text": "„In leichtem und trocknem Erdreich geht Schnittlauch mit jedem Jahr mehr zurück; eine dauernde, kräftige Schnittlauchanlage läßt sich hier nur erhalten, wenn von Zeit zu Zeit Samen oder Pflanzen aus kräftigem Boden neu bezogen werden.“"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "08e95f6c-461a-495b-9440-16e0374022ed",
                                                "sense_id": "5f07de70-9f6b-39c8-9fae-c3be0026d4de",
                                                "text": "Ein Mann, der eines Nachmittags müde nach Hause kam, hätte gern ein Stück Butterbrod mit Schnittlauch darauf gegessen, oder etwas von einem geräucherten Bug."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e0cdc73e-e021-455a-9de8-43269bbdc26c",
                                                "sense_id": "5f07de70-9f6b-39c8-9fae-c3be0026d4de",
                                                "text": "„Außerdem legte er um das Haus herum Gemüsebeete an und pflanzte Petersilie, Dill, Schnittlauch, Mohrrüben, Kopfsalat und Fleischtomaten.“"
                                            }
                                        ],
                                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "20bbc772-f71f-4dc5-b8e0-c4c987ae9ad0",
                        "source_entry_id": "2dd45744-32e0-3d53-85e1-e3d1653e93fa",
                        "source_sense_id": "0a8d1502-5b5d-3c81-9496-79e33bf1bb0d",
                        "source_sense_description": "en 2. (groene sprietjes van) looksoort Allium schoenoprasum",
                        "target_lang_word": "chive",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "chive",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "76c92cfe-b03f-30da-9441-f70926b42b67",
                                        "entry_id": "3919454c-9044-3d0d-b54e-dd9ee8a90442",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A knife."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "482b55e1-1f08-4390-8eb1-285d77ac61d2",
                                                "sense_id": "76c92cfe-b03f-30da-9441-f70926b42b67",
                                                "text": "For when that he hath nubbed as, / And our friends tip him no cole, / He takes his chive and cuts us down, / And tips us into a hole."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "929d31ed-c3a1-40f0-8ff8-cf554131a31d",
                                                "sense_id": "76c92cfe-b03f-30da-9441-f70926b42b67",
                                                "text": "None of us know'd then—though the grabbing at Nan Turner's came off that very night—as Polly was the cause o' that 'ere, till it vos blown here at the Gate by some of the coves. Vell, she nammused, as you may guess, but fust poor old Madge Rhodes got a chive in her breather from Black Gil."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4a1ba3d2-1f0f-42c4-9c33-847417bcf234",
                                                "sense_id": "76c92cfe-b03f-30da-9441-f70926b42b67",
                                                "text": "On the Boxing Day after I came out I got stabbed in the chest by a pal of mine who had done a schooling. We was out with one another all the day getting drunk, so he took a liberty with me, and I landed him one on the conk (nose), so we had a fight, and he put the chive (knife) into me."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "66db9779-0a43-484c-950d-e6f89f8a76b6",
                                                "sense_id": "76c92cfe-b03f-30da-9441-f70926b42b67",
                                                "text": "I guyed, but the reeler he gave me hot beef, / And a scuff came about me and hollered; / I pulled out a chive but I soon same to grief, / And with screws and a james I was collared."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "c2b05e5a-b896-3a7b-ab41-78c442175e74",
                                        "entry_id": "3919454c-9044-3d0d-b54e-dd9ee8a90442",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A file."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "7473a351-79ff-3675-9896-bcbf885f483f",
                                        "entry_id": "3919454c-9044-3d0d-b54e-dd9ee8a90442",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A saw."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "chive",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "f144d802-1744-3933-b7ac-78286feecf09",
                                        "entry_id": "f9def4f4-2ec3-3667-86de-9417a279c9cd",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To stab."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "377516ce-085b-4a6e-a81d-b884db50cde3",
                                                "sense_id": "f144d802-1744-3933-b7ac-78286feecf09",
                                                "text": "Adieu to Haul-Cly, adieu to stopping Coaches, and adieu to all the hurry-scurry of Foot-Scampering, filing, chiving, milling, and sneaking[…]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "975a7f93-32b6-4865-9126-8e752577da8b",
                                                "sense_id": "f144d802-1744-3933-b7ac-78286feecf09",
                                                "text": "He was as good a man as Jacky at any weapon that could be named, and if Jacky were game for a chiving (stabbing) match, he (Kavanagh) was ready for him."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9cd80139-ba9b-416e-834d-bae2b2ae2084",
                                                "sense_id": "f144d802-1744-3933-b7ac-78286feecf09",
                                                "text": "After the place got well where I was chived, me and another screwed a place at Stoke Newington"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "e8ea92e5-f348-34a7-adcf-09142c27d152",
                                        "entry_id": "f9def4f4-2ec3-3667-86de-9417a279c9cd",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To cut."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "chive",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "dcbd6c19-81d3-31d7-ba51-4e9b4a07b054",
                                        "entry_id": "e8358ae8-5ae4-3959-a4b0-0fd87c24ce17",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A perennial plant, Allium schoenoprasum, related to the onion."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "28bf549b-491e-3846-a248-009edb4972a0",
                                        "entry_id": "e8358ae8-5ae4-3959-a4b0-0fd87c24ce17",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The leaves of this plant used as a herb."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "attributive",
                                            "in-plural"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "339a84fb-4e8e-4436-9e51-85539e9cd98f",
                                                "sense_id": "28bf549b-491e-3846-a248-009edb4972a0",
                                                "text": "Chive herb. (attributive use)"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "27864c54-d69d-4dd5-8e91-35252fc90469",
                                                "sense_id": "28bf549b-491e-3846-a248-009edb4972a0",
                                                "text": "What a splendid pie, pizza-pizza pie / Every minute, every second, buy, buy, buy, buy, buy / Pepperoni and green peppers, mushrooms, olive, chives"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "0d0a2e8a-4701-395a-9ce7-55a7306371f0",
                                        "entry_id": "e8358ae8-5ae4-3959-a4b0-0fd87c24ce17",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The spring onion; the green onion; the scallion."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "Trinidad-and-Tobago",
                                            "dialectal"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "chive",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "cb333ee0-a441-38a1-a005-f186b8c0b349",
                                        "entry_id": "cbc0a800-967c-34aa-9daa-a18ee238f7b7",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A piece cut off; sliver."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "060fbbde-e117-3af3-97e3-ffd0e8ca8fb8",
                                        "entry_id": "cbc0a800-967c-34aa-9daa-a18ee238f7b7",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The filament which supports the anther of a flower; stamen, especially of saffron."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "af2dff76-c4b6-4c3b-b9ca-fd2bc82e1512",
                                                "sense_id": "060fbbde-e117-3af3-97e3-ffd0e8ca8fb8",
                                                "text": "1577, Raphael Holinshed et al., The Chronicles of England, Scotlande, and Ireland, London: John Hunne, Book 3, Chapter 14, “Of English Saffron,”\n[…] in the place wher he bled, Saffron was after found to grow, whereupon the people séeyng the color of the chiue as it stoode, (although I doubt not but it grewe there long before) adiudged it to come to the bloude of Crocus, and therefore they gaue it his name."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0536b2a7-076c-490d-be3c-7287d43c1929",
                                                "sense_id": "060fbbde-e117-3af3-97e3-ffd0e8ca8fb8",
                                                "text": "[…] to abate, and allay the fulnesse of red, we doe not see white vsed (as a colour too remote) but rather yellow, and that so farre-forth as some doe grinde a Chiue of Saffron with Vermillion, to make it the more pleasant, whereas white in like proportion mixed, would dimne, and decay it […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e7ca1692-2787-4899-a816-e2f259695a88",
                                                "sense_id": "060fbbde-e117-3af3-97e3-ffd0e8ca8fb8",
                                                "text": "The chiues or threds in the middle of the floure are ſometimes of a reddiſh, or of a blackiſh colour."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "31103640-9e47-4afe-925b-40c88c2842e6",
                                                "sense_id": "060fbbde-e117-3af3-97e3-ffd0e8ca8fb8",
                                                "text": "The Saint, to which the most he prayes\nAnd offers Incense Nights and dayes,\nThe Lady of the Lobster is,\nWhose foot-pace he doth stroak & kisse;\nAnd, humbly, chives of Saffron brings,\nFor his most cheerfull offerings."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "c6dc700f-1c3a-4dc9-8560-d37424f6fd82",
                        "source_entry_id": "2dd45744-32e0-3d53-85e1-e3d1653e93fa",
